#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180783 :tutorial/content "To determine the slope of a line that is given in standard form, we need to convert the equation to slope-intercept form.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the slope of the following line:\n\n$$\n2x + y = 8\n$$\n\nTo write this equation in slope-intercept form, we solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y &= 8 \\\\\n2x + y - 2x &= 8 - 2x \\\\\ny &= 8 - 2x \\\\\ny &= -2x + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept equation, we can tell that the slope of the line is $m=-2$." :tutorial/id #uuid "3550ae2f-df69-52cc-b33a-14e8f2bb2d8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8697 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180784 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the $x$- and $y$-intercepts of the following line:\n\n$$\n2x + y = 8\n$$\n\nLet's find each of them in turn.\n\n- To find the $x$-intercept, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation and solve for $x{:}$\n$2x + (0)|= 8; 2x|= 8; \\frac{2x}{2}|= \\frac{8}{2}; x|= 4$\nTherefore, the line intersects the $x$-axis at the point $(4,0)$.\n\n- To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation and solve for $y{:}$\n$2(0) + y|= 8; y|= 8$\nTherefore, the line intersects the $y$-axis at the point $(0,8)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "c500072d-6462-54c2-8c16-3c8f68bdbbfe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8699 :tutorial/title "Determining Intercepts of Lines Given in Standard Form"}]}
